            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Holds a 1-D tensor and writes values into it from index, value pairs, keeping everything else as it was.\n\nSend it 3 0.8 and slot 3 becomes 0.8; send 3 0.8 7 0.2 and two slots change at once. It remembers between messages, so values arriving one at a time, from different nodes and at different rates, build up into a single tensor that always holds the latest of each - per-joint readings that arrive separately and something downstream that wants them all together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.scatter_hold <length: int>\nDefault length 16.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.scatter_hold 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nlist of index value pairs:\nAlternating positions and values: index, value, index, value... (a list, an array or a tensor). Writes them and sends the whole tensor. An odd number of entries is ignored - it cannot be read as pairs. Indices are rounded down to whole numbers and pulled into range: anything below 0 writes slot 0 and anything past the end writes the LAST slot, so a stray index quietly overwrites an end slot rather than failing.\n\nlength of target tensor (drag int, also an inlet):\nHow many slots. Changing it makes a new tensor of zeros - what was held is lost. Minimum 1.\n\nclear (button, also an inlet):\nSets every slot back to zero and sends the cleared tensor, so a display downstream clears at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe whole held tensor (float32) after each write - not just the slots that changed - so anything downstream always sees the complete current state. Each send is a copy: a tensor already sent is not changed by later writes, so a node further on that keeps one (var, a buffer) keeps the state at that moment.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe first message is sent once on load. Click either message: each changes only its own slots and the rest keep\nwhat they had. Drag the slider to write its value into the slot named by the int box (3 to start); pak sends the pair on every move, so that one slot follows the slider. Type another number in the int box to move the slider to another slot - the old one keeps its last value. Click clear on the node and the strip goes back to zeros at once; move the slider or click a message to start building it up again.\n\nRELATED:\nt.scatter writes a whole source tensor into a target at given positions, without holding anything. np.edit writes into a region of a numpy array. t.index_select reads values out by position.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 920,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Holds a 1-D tensor and writes values into it from index, value pairs, keeping everything else as it was.\n\nSend it 3 0.8 and slot 3 becomes 0.8; send 3 0.8 7 0.2 and two slots change at once. It remembers between messages, so values arriving one at a time, from different nodes and at different rates, build up into a single tensor that always holds the latest of each - per-joint readings that arrive separately and something downstream that wants them all together.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.scatter_hold <length: int>\nDefault length 16.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.scatter_hold 8\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nlist of index value pairs:\nAlternating positions and values: index, value, index, value... (a list, an array or a tensor). Writes them and sends the whole tensor. An odd number of entries is ignored - it cannot be read as pairs. Indices are rounded down to whole numbers and pulled into range: anything below 0 writes slot 0 and anything past the end writes the LAST slot, so a stray index quietly overwrites an end slot rather than failing.\n\nlength of target tensor (drag int, also an inlet):\nHow many slots. Changing it makes a new tensor of zeros - what was held is lost. Minimum 1.\n\nclear (button, also an inlet):\nSets every slot back to zero and sends the cleared tensor, so a display downstream clears at once.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe whole held tensor (float32) after each write - not just the slots that changed - so anything downstream always sees the complete current state. Each send is a copy: a tensor already sent is not changed by later writes, so a node further on that keeps one (var, a buffer) keeps the state at that moment.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe first message is sent once on load. Click either message: each changes only its own slots and the rest keep\nwhat they had. Drag the slider to write its value into the slot named by the int box (3 to start); pak sends the pair on every move, so that one slot follows the slider. Type another number in the int box to move the slider to another slot - the old one keeps its last value. Click clear on the node and the strip goes back to zeros at once; move the slider or click a message to start building it up again.\n\nRELATED:\nt.scatter writes a whole source tensor into a target at given positions, without holding anything. np.edit writes into a region of a numpy array. t.index_select reads values out by position."
        },
